  <article class=3D"guide-prose">
    <h2 data-num=3D"01" id=3D"overview">Overview</h2>
<p>This guide provides FFmpeg parameters and quality settings for x264 vide=
o encoding across different frame rates. The x264 encoder is widely support=
ed and offers excellent quality-to-filesize ratios.</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> These recommendations are somewhat a matter of op=
inion and based on community consensus and testing. Your optimal settings m=
ay vary depending on your source material and specific goals.</p>
<h2 data-num=3D"02" id=3D"ffmpeg-parameters-by-frame-rate">FFmpeg Parameter=
s by Frame Rate</h2>
<h3>Medium Preset (Default)</h3>
<p>The encoder settings vary based on video frame rate. Each configuration =
specifies keyframe intervals and B-frame counts optimized for that framerat=
e:</p>
<h4>24 fps</h4>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">-x264-params keyint=3D240:bframes=3D6:ref=
=3D4:me=3Dumh:subme=3D9:no-fast-pskip=3D1:b-adapt=3D2:aq-mode=3D2
</code></pre></div>
<h4>25 fps</h4>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">-x264-params keyint=3D250:bframes=3D6:ref=
=3D4:me=3Dumh:subme=3D9:no-fast-pskip=3D1:b-adapt=3D2:aq-mode=3D2
</code></pre></div>
<h4>30 fps</h4>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">-x264-params keyint=3D300:bframes=3D6:ref=
=3D4:me=3Dumh:subme=3D9:no-fast-pskip=3D1:b-adapt=3D2:aq-mode=3D2
</code></pre></div>
<h4>50 fps</h4>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">-x264-params keyint=3D500:bframes=3D7:ref=
=3D4:me=3Dumh:subme=3D9:no-fast-pskip=3D1:b-adapt=3D2:aq-mode=3D2
</code></pre></div>
<h4>60 fps</h4>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">-x264-params keyint=3D600:bframes=3D7:ref=
=3D4:me=3Dumh:subme=3D9:no-fast-pskip=3D1:b-adapt=3D2:aq-mode=3D2
</code></pre></div>
<h3>Very Slow Preset</h3>
<p>For maximum quality encoding with the <code>veryslow</code> preset, I on=
ly recommend setting two additional parameters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>keyint</strong> - Set to 10=C3=97 your frame rate</li>
<li><strong>aq-mode</strong> - Set to 2 for better quality distribution</li=
>
</ul>
<p>The <code>veryslow</code> preset already includes optimal values for mos=
t other parameters, so additional tuning is generally unnecessary.</p>
<h4>Example (30 fps)</h4>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">-preset veryslow -x264-params keyint=3D300:=
aq-mode=3D2
</code></pre></div>
<p>For other frame rates, simply adjust the keyint value accordingly (e.g.,=
 240 for 24fps, 600 for 60fps).</p>
<h2 data-num=3D"03" id=3D"quality-settings-crf-values">Quality Settings (CR=
F Values)</h2>
<p>The x264 encoder uses <strong>Constant Rate Factor (CRF)</strong> as a r=
ate control mode that prioritizes consistent visual quality over fixed bitr=
ate. The encoder dynamically allocates more bits to complex scenes while us=
ing fewer for simpler ones, resulting in variable bitrate output.</p>
<h3>CRF Scale</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Range</strong>: 0 to 51, where lower values mean higher quality=
 (larger files) and higher values mean lower quality (smaller files)</li>
<li><strong>Default</strong>: 23 offers a good balance of quality and effic=
iency</li>
<li><strong>Lossless</strong>: 0 for lossless encoding (8-bit only; use <co=
de>-qp 0</code> for 10-bit)</li>
<li>A change of =C2=B16 in CRF typically halves or doubles the bitrate</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> CRF values are not directly comparable across cod=
ecs (x264 CRF 23 =E2=89=88 x265 CRF 28).</p>
<h3>Recommended CRF by Purpose and Resolution</h3>
<p>Higher resolutions can often tolerate higher CRF values due to better co=
mpression efficiency at scale:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Purpose/Use Case</th>
<th>CRF Range</th>
<th>Typical Resolution</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>High-Quality Archival</strong></td>
<td>16=E2=80=9320</td>
<td>Any (720p+)</td>
<td>Near-transparent quality; larger files. For source preservation or upsc=
ale viewing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Visually Transparent</strong></td>
<td>18=E2=80=9323</td>
<td>480p=E2=80=931080p</td>
<td>Indistinguishable from source to most viewers; CRF 18 for demanding con=
tent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>General/Balanced</strong></td>
<td>20=E2=80=9324</td>
<td>720p=E2=80=931080p</td>
<td>Good for Blu-ray rips, personal libraries. CRF 21=E2=80=9323 recommende=
d baseline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Efficient Compression</strong></td>
<td>24=E2=80=9328</td>
<td>1080p=E2=80=934K</td>
<td>Smaller files with minimal visible loss; suitable for web sharing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Low-Bitrate/Streaming</strong></td>
<td>28+</td>
<td>480p=E2=80=93720p</td>
<td>Mobile/web distribution; test for artifacts. Avoid exceeding 30</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Resolution-Specific Guidelines</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard Definition (480p/576p)</strong>: CRF 18=E2=80=9322 hel=
ps combat upscaling artifacts on HD displays</li>
<li><strong>720p HD</strong>: CRF 19=E2=80=9323, with 19=E2=80=9320 for mov=
ies to maintain detail</li>
<li><strong>1080p Full HD</strong>: CRF 20=E2=80=9324, the sweet spot for m=
ost consumer encodes</li>
<li><strong>4K UHD (2160p)</strong>: CRF 22=E2=80=9328, leveraging codec ef=
ficiency at high resolutions</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommended baseline:</strong> CRF 21 is a suggested starting po=
int. For live-action content, apply film tuning (<code>-tune film</code>). =
Always test a short clip and evaluate quality before encoding your full vid=
eo.</p>
<h2 data-num=3D"04" id=3D"essential-x264-parameters">Essential x264 Paramet=
ers</h2>
<p>These settings consistently improve encoding quality:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Motion estimation</strong> (<code>me=3Dumh</code>) - Uses uneve=
n multi-hexagon search for better motion vectors</li>
<li><strong>Subpixel refinement</strong> (<code>subme=3D9</code>) - Maximum=
 quality subpixel motion estimation</li>
<li><strong>B-frame adaptation</strong> (<code>b-adapt=3D2</code>) - Optima=
l B-frame placement</li>
<li><strong>Adaptive quantization</strong> (<code>aq-mode=3D2</code>) - Bet=
ter quality distribution across the frame</li>
<li><strong>P-frame skip disabled</strong> (<code>no-fast-pskip=3D1</code>)=
 - Prevents quality loss from skipped macroblocks</li>
<li><strong>Reference frames</strong> (<code>ref=3D4</code>) - Number of pr=
evious frames to reference</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-num=3D"05" id=3D"complete-example-command">Complete Example Comman=
d</h2>
<p>Here's a complete FFmpeg command for 30fps content:</p>
<div class=3D"snippet"><div class=3D"snippet__bar"><span>$ bash</span><butt=
on type=3D"button" class=3D"snippet__copy">=E2=A7=89 copy</button></div><pr=
e><code class=3D"language-bash">ffmpeg -i input.mp4 \
  -c:v libx264 \
  -crf 21 \
  -tune film \
  -x264-params keyint=3D300:bframes=3D6:ref=3D4:me=3Dumh:subme=3D9:no-fast-=
pskip=3D1:b-adapt=3D2:aq-mode=3D2 \
  -c:a aac -b:a 192k \
  output.mp4
</code></pre></div>
<h2 data-num=3D"06" id=3D"notes">Notes</h2>
<ul>
<li>The keyint value should generally be set to 10=C3=97 the frame rate for=
 optimal seeking</li>
<li>Higher bframes values (7 for 50/60fps) can improve compression efficien=
cy at higher framerates</li>
<li>The tune parameter should match your content type (film, animation, gra=
in, etc.)</li>
</ul>

  </article>

